  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) has a time field called `timestamp`. The HTTP response code is stored as text in `response`, with an exact-match version in `response.keyword`. Other fields include `bytes`, `clientip`, `host`, `request`, `url`, `agent`, `geo.*` and `machine.os`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-f781ad75 Web logs' (id `s4-f781ad75-web-logs-04e8cd269f68`) with two panels:\n   - a line chart of request count over `timestamp`\n   - a bar chart of request count split by `response.keyword`, showing the top 20 codes\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-f781ad75 5xx errors' (id `dd2f9619-175d-4236-9da9-908117aa11e9`) as an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It checks every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive in 5 minutes. It counts requests whose `response.keyword` is from \"500\" to \"599\", inclusive. Because the code is stored as text, that range is compared alphabetically, which works correctly for normal three-digit codes. The rule was enabled when created.\n4. **Edit and check:** I renamed the response-code panel to 'Responses by code'; the update succeeded, but I didn't reload the dashboard to look at the new title. Reading the rule back shows it is enabled, with status \"ok\".\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
